# Trip an adjacent goblin six times and record every attack of opportunity the
# trips raise (bd inc-83dw).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#   INCURSION_TRIP_AOO_PROBE=1 \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/trip-aoo.keys 5
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. src/Fight.cpp, case AD_TRIP, passed e.EActor to
# Creature::ProvokeAoO where it must pass e.EVictim. ProvokeAoO(c) attacks with
# c and defends with this, so the tripper became both and hit himself with his
# own weapon. The probe writes one line per accepted attack of opportunity
# naming its actor, its victim and whether they are one creature.
# tools/check_trip_aoo.sh reads logs/tripaoo.log and counts both columns.
#
# WHY A GOBLIN. The AD_TRIP case refuses a target that is prone, amorphous,
# incorporeal or M_NOLIMBS, and SAttack's A_TRIP refuses an aerial target and a
# swarm. A goblin is none of those, it is the same monster four other checks
# already summon, and it is weak enough that a level 1 orc warrior is not
# killed by the fight it starts.
#
# WHY FREEZE MONSTERS STAYS ON. dequ-setup.keys turns it on, and the game's own
# help for that switch says frozen monsters "are still a part of the world and
# can be affected by spells, take attacks of opportunity and so forth"
# (src/Tables.cpp). src/Main.cpp confirms it: the freeze skips ChooseAction
# only, while the refill of every creature's AoO counter runs in the outer loop
# every 60 segments by map iteration. So the goblin can answer the trip without
# ever taking a turn of its own.
#
# WHY FOUR GOBLINS AND NOT ONE. A landed trip leaves its target PRONE, and both
# SAttack's A_TRIP and the AD_TRIP case refuse a prone target outright, so the
# same goblin cannot be tripped twice. Each trip therefore needs a goblin of its
# own, and they stand east, north, south and west of the character. The trip
# must also WIN its opposed check to reach Creature::Damage, so four attempts
# give the run several chances at that.
#
# WHY THE FOUR ARE SUMMONED FIRST AND TRIPPED AFTERWARDS. A freshly summoned
# monster carries no attack of opportunity. The counter is filled for every
# creature on the map by the outer loop in src/Main.cpp, once every 60 segments,
# and nothing fills it at birth. A first draft summoned each goblin and tripped
# it in the next keystroke; three trips landed and not one attack of opportunity
# followed, because every target still held a zero counter. So all four are
# placed, the character waits, and only then does he trip. The waits are what
# make the measurement possible and they MUST NOT be removed.
#
# THE SELF-ATTACK CAN KILL THE TRIPPER, which is the defect at its worst, so
# the screen is dumped after every trip. A run that ends early still leaves its
# evidence behind, and the probe log is written and flushed line by line.

@include dequ-setup.keys

# Summon a goblin one square east.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "goblin"
RIGHT
ENTER
@dump:goblin-east-placed

# Summon a goblin one square north.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "goblin"
UP
ENTER
@dump:goblin-north-placed

# Summon a goblin one square south.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "goblin"
DOWN
ENTER
@dump:goblin-south-placed

# Summon a goblin one square west.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "goblin"
LEFT
ENTER
@dump:goblin-west-placed

# Wait. '.' is Wait One Turn (src/Tables.cpp). Eight of them carry the game
# well past the next 60-segment boundary, so every goblin holds an attack of
# opportunity before the first trip. The goblins are frozen and cannot use the
# time against the character.
.*8
@dump:after-wait

# Trip the east goblin. 'c' opens the Tactical Options menu and Trip asks
# for a direction. @until re-presses 'c' if a message prompt swallowed the
# first one, which it did on the second trip of an earlier draft.
@until "Tactical Options" c
@choose "Trip"
RIGHT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:trip-east

# Trip the north goblin. 'c' opens the Tactical Options menu and Trip asks
# for a direction. @until re-presses 'c' if a message prompt swallowed the
# first one, which it did on the second trip of an earlier draft.
@until "Tactical Options" c
@choose "Trip"
UP
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:trip-north

# Trip the south goblin. 'c' opens the Tactical Options menu and Trip asks
# for a direction. @until re-presses 'c' if a message prompt swallowed the
# first one, which it did on the second trip of an earlier draft.
@until "Tactical Options" c
@choose "Trip"
DOWN
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:trip-south

# Trip the west goblin. 'c' opens the Tactical Options menu and Trip asks
# for a direction. @until re-presses 'c' if a message prompt swallowed the
# first one, which it did on the second trip of an earlier draft.
@until "Tactical Options" c
@choose "Trip"
LEFT
@while "Attack the" y
@dump:trip-west

@quit
